WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.738
A bipartite graph has two sides

2
00:00:02.738 --> 00:00:05.024
and every edge joins the two

3
00:00:05.024 --> 00:00:08.300
users and products, or authors and papers.

4
00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:11.425
A butterfly is two vertices on each side

5
00:00:11.425 --> 00:00:13.185
joined by all four edges

6
00:00:13.253 --> 00:00:15.013
the smallest dense pattern

7
00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:16.570
such a graph can have.

8
00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.800
ParButterfly, a framework from 2020

9
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.360
counts butterflies in parallel.

10
00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.265
A wedge is a path of two edges

11
00:00:25.265 --> 00:00:27.740
with both ends on the same side.

12
00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:30.340
Two vertices that share c neighbors

13
00:00:30.405 --> 00:00:32.012
are the ends of c wedges

14
00:00:32.077 --> 00:00:33.813
and any two of those wedges

15
00:00:33.877 --> 00:00:34.970
form a butterfly.

16
00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:39.025
So they share c times c minus one, over two

17
00:00:39.025 --> 00:00:40.120
butterflies.

18
00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.438
Counting comes down to collecting

19
00:00:42.498 --> 00:00:43.750
wedges by their ends.

20
00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:46.724
The framework first ranks the vertices

21
00:00:46.785 --> 00:00:49.118
and keeps only the wedges whose center

22
00:00:49.179 --> 00:00:51.450
and far end rank after the first end.

23
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.600
So each butterfly is counted once.

24
00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.670
The order decides how many wedges there are.

25
00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.140
Side order ranks one whole side first.

26
00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:04.078
Degree order ranks the hubs first

27
00:01:04.078 --> 00:01:06.210
so hubs are seldom centers.

28
00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:10.264
Pause and predict: on a graph with big hubs

29
00:01:10.264 --> 00:01:12.790
which order needs fewer wedges?

30
00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:19.105
Our two graphs have forty vertices on each

31
00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.540
side and three hundred twenty edges.

32
00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:23.230
On the uniform graph

33
00:01:23.294 --> 00:01:25.271
the best side order needs fewer

34
00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:26.930
wedges than degree order.

35
00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:28.560
On the skewed graph

36
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.440
degree order needs forty-four percent fewer.

37
00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.224
The paper's rule takes degree order when

38
00:01:34.285 --> 00:01:36.110
it saves at least ten percent.

39
00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:39.375
The task removes the routine that

40
00:01:39.441 --> 00:01:41.030
returns the exact total.

41
00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.012
Only the count is checked

42
00:01:43.012 --> 00:01:45.460
so the agent may choose its own order.

43
00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.449
On eight graphs of about two million edges

44
00:01:48.449 --> 00:01:51.026
on four threads, the count must be exact

45
00:01:51.087 --> 00:01:53.235
and the code more than five percent

46
00:01:53.296 --> 00:01:55.321
faster than the authors' parallel

47
00:01:55.383 --> 00:01:56.610
code on every graph.

48
00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.694
With an FDI of minus eleven point two

49
00:01:59.694 --> 00:02:02.526
this is the easiest task of the benchmark

50
00:02:02.526 --> 00:02:05.030
and fifteen of sixteen models passed.

51
00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.646
The best runs counted about six times

52
00:02:07.708 --> 00:02:09.300
faster than the reference.

53
00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:12.897
Claude Opus 5.5 chose a strategy per graph

54
00:02:12.897 --> 00:02:15.012
the side with fewer wedges

55
00:02:15.012 --> 00:02:16.598
or on skewed graphs

56
00:02:16.598 --> 00:02:19.090
a degree order if it is cheaper.

57
00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:21.524
That ends the HPC lecture

58
00:02:21.524 --> 00:02:22.950
with three lessons.

59
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.150
Work first, then threads

60
00:02:25.224 --> 00:02:27.444
each paper cuts the total work

61
00:02:27.518 --> 00:02:29.220
before it adds threads.

62
00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:31.090
Every workload counts

63
00:02:31.159 --> 00:02:34.614
the best scores on RegionsSort and Parallel DBSCAN

64
00:02:34.683 --> 00:02:37.170
each failed on one of twelve inputs.

65
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:40.240
And a pass is decided in its own run.

66
00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:41.592
As an exercise

67
00:02:41.659 --> 00:02:44.465
count the wedges a single vertex of degree

68
00:02:44.532 --> 00:02:46.670
forty would produce as a center.

69
00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:48.718
The interactive notes have

70
00:02:48.783 --> 00:02:50.220
every program we used.

71
00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:53.850
Next time: Lecture 3, architecture.
